src/test/modules/test_checksums/spec/README

A formal model of the data checksum state machine
=================================================
This directory contains two machine-checked models of the code that reads and
modifies the data checksum state of a cluster: a Lean 4 development in Spec/,
and a TLA+ transcription of it in tla/.  They are specifications, not tests:
nothing here is built or run by "make check" or by meson, and no PostgreSQL
source file depends on either of them.

The two are complementary, and the division of labour is the one argued for at
the bottom of this file:

    Spec/ (Lean 4)   safety, for an *arbitrary* number of backends and pages,
                     plus the audit of checksum_barriers[] that will catch a
                     future edit to that table
    tla/  (TLA+)     liveness, counterexample traces, and an independent check
                     that the Lean inductive invariant really is inductive

The informal specification they formalize is the header comment of
src/backend/postmaster/datachecksum_state.c, in particular the two rules

    all backends MUST calculate and write data checksums during all states
    except off; MUST validate checksums only in the 'on' state.

and the claim that the two intermediate states plus the procsignal barrier
handshake are enough to make those rules hold while the state is changing
underneath running backends.


What is proved
==============
Two properties, for every reachable cluster state, for an arbitrary number of
backends and an arbitrary number of pages (Spec/Safety.lean):

  reachable_writeSafe
      If any process verifies data checksums, then every process is writing
      them.  This is the first rule above, stated over the readers
      DataChecksumsNeedVerify() and DataChecksumsNeedWrite() rather than over
      the states themselves.

  reachable_readSafe
      If any process verifies data checksums, then every page on disk carries
      one.

  no_spurious_failure
      The corollary the feature is really about: PageIsVerified() never reports
      a checksum failure on a page the cluster itself wrote.

Alongside those, a handful of finite audits of the checksum_barriers table are
proved by evaluation, and are the first thing to look at if that table is ever
edited:

  barrierTable_length             the table still has nine entries
  barrierTable_mixSafe            no entry pairs a verifying backend with a
                                  non-writing one
  barrierTable_no_direct_toggle   there is no direct on/off edge either way
  barrierTable_pairwiseSafe       the full 4^3 case check behind write safety
  coordinatorEdges_absorbable     every transition the launcher and xlog2_redo()
                                  drive is accepted by
                                  AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier(), so no barrier
                                  can livelock on the ereport(ERROR) retry path

Three negative results show the audits have teeth, i.e. that they would fail if
the design were weakened: onOff_shortcut_unsafe, offOn_shortcut_unsafe and
twoStateDesign_unsafe.  The last one is the formal statement of why the two
'inprogress-*' states have to exist at all.

Finally, Spec/Trace.lean exhibits a concrete nine-step execution of a
two-backend cluster that performs a complete online enable, so the results above
are not vacuous.  Its state c8 is the situation the whole design exists to
survive: one backend has absorbed the barrier and verifies checksums while
another has not and merely writes them.


What is model-checked
=====================
tla/DataChecksums.tla is a transcription of Spec/Cluster.lean: the same four
state components, the same six transitions, the same initial predicate.  The
Lean development already proves safety, so the TLA+ module is aimed at the three
things a proof assistant is bad at.  Each has a .cfg file of its own.

  DataChecksums.cfg
      The main run.  Re-checks TypeOK, WriteSafe, ReadSafe, NoSpuriousFailure
      and the barrier table audit for three backends and two pages, and checks
      the liveness property.  A few hundred states; finishes instantly.

  Inductive.cfg
      Not a check of the C code but of the Lean proof.  It uses Inv -- Lean's
      inductive invariant, transcribed field for field -- as the *initial*
      predicate, so TLC starts from every state satisfying Inv rather than from
      the reachable ones, and confirms each transition preserves it.  That is
      precisely the obligation Spec/Safety.lean's Inv.step discharges, so the
      two developments cross-validate each other.  A reported search depth of 1
      is the check passing: every successor of an Inv state is already one.

  BrokenTable.cfg
      Adds the on -> off shortcut to checksum_barriers[] and prints the
      interleaving that breaks WriteSafe.  This is onOff_shortcut_unsafe made
      executable; Lean can tell you the design is broken, TLC tells you how.

  Livelock.cfg
      Lets the coordinator drive an edge that is *not* in checksum_barriers[],
      and reports a lasso in which one process is left behind for ever.  This
      is the property the Lean development cannot even state: a rejected edge
      makes AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier() raise an error, procsignal resets the
      bit and retries, and WaitForProcSignalBarrier() never returns.

The liveness property is

  BarrierRetires == ~Quiescent ~> Quiescent

"whenever some process is behind, the handshake eventually retires".  Only the
two actions that make up that handshake are fair -- AdvanceControl, and Absorb
per backend.  BackendStart deliberately is not: a running backend cannot call
InitLocalDataChecksumState(), only a newly forked one does, and no progress
argument may lean on new connections arriving.  AdvanceShared is not fair
either, so nothing here claims a requested enable ever finishes; that would need
the launcher's goal to be modelled, and is noted below as future work.

Writing the model down this way turned up one thing the Lean development had
missed.  Lean's advanceShared rule requires the new state to be a barrier table
edge by construction, so the two tables agreeing is an *assumption* there and
coordinatorEdges_absorbable is a separate side theorem.  The TLA+ module keeps
BarrierTable and CoordinatorEdges apart, which is what the C code actually does:
SetDataChecksums*() and xlog2_redo() assign to XLogCtl->data_checksum_version
directly, and nothing checks that assignment against the table.  Under
Livelock.cfg, TLC finds not only the expected livelock but also a WriteSafe
violation, because InitLocalDataChecksumState() copies the shared value verbatim
with no validation at all -- so a backend forked after the illegal transition
adopts the illegal target immediately, while an older one is still verifying.
An edge outside checksum_barriers[] is therefore a data-corruption bug, not
merely a hang.  That is a stronger reason to keep coordinatorEdges_absorbable
true than the Lean development on its own gives.


What is modelled
================
The model covers the three copies of the state and every function that reads or
writes them.  The Lean and TLA+ names are listed together because they are
transcriptions of each other.

  Lean (Spec/)                     C
  -------------------------------  ------------------------------------------
  ChecksumState                    ChecksumStateType (storage/checksum.h)
  ChecksumState.toVersion          PG_DATA_CHECKSUM_* encoding
  Cluster.shared                   XLogCtl->data_checksum_version
  Cluster.control                  ControlFile->data_checksum_version
  Cluster.localState               LocalDataChecksumState, one per process
  Cluster.onDisk                   whether the page on disk carries a checksum

  needWrite                        DataChecksumsNeedWrite()
  needVerify                       DataChecksumsNeedVerify()
                                   DataChecksumsOn/Off/InProgressOn() are
                                   definable from Cluster.shared and are not
                                   given names of their own

  barrierTable                     checksum_barriers[9]
  absorbAllowed                    the validation in
                                   AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier()
  coordinatorEdges                 the transitions SetDataChecksums*() and
                                   xlog2_redo() drive
  recover                          the end-of-recovery fixups in StartupXLOG()

  Step.backendStart                InitLocalDataChecksumState()
  Step.absorb                      AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier()
  Step.writePage                   PageSetChecksum(), and by extension the
                                   XLogHintBitIsNeeded() paths, and the
                                   datachecksums worker rewriting a page
  Step.advanceShared               the XLogCtl assignment under info_lck in
                                   SetDataChecksumsOnInProgress(),
                                   SetDataChecksumsOn(), SetDataChecksumsOff()
                                   and xlog2_redo()
  Step.advanceControl              ControlFile assignment plus
                                   UpdateControlFile() under ControlFileLock
  Step.restart                     crash or immediate shutdown, then
                                   XLOGShmemInit() plus StartupXLOG()
  Init                             initdb and offline pg_checksums

The TLA+ names are the same modulo capitalisation and the Cluster./Step.
qualifiers -- Cluster.shared is shared, Step.absorb is Absorb, and so on -- so
the table above serves for both.  The one deliberate divergence is
CoordinatorEdges, described under "What is model-checked" above.

Reads are deliberately not steps.  Every consumer of the state --
PageIsVerified(), PageSetChecksum(), XLogHintBitIsNeeded() and its callers in
bufmgr.c, pruneheap.c, visibilitymap.c and freespace.c,
backup_checksums_verifiable(), show_data_checksums() -- is a pure function of
one process' local copy, so "a read misbehaves" is a property of a state rather
than of a transition.  That is what makes the safety statements above say
everything there is to say about the readers.

Splitting advanceShared from advanceControl is what exposes the window that
SetDataChecksumsOn() reasons about in its comment:

    Update the controlfile before waiting since if we have an immediate
    shutdown while waiting we want to come back up with checksums enabled.

A Step.restart may happen in that window, and the invariant fields
controlOnShared, controlOnWrites and controlOnPages are exactly what is needed
to show the cluster comes back up safely.

The key insight of the proof is that the obvious invariant -- "every local state
is barrier-compatible with the shared state" -- is too weak.  It admits a
population where one backend is at 'on' and another at 'off', which is precisely
what must not happen.  The extra fact needed is Inv.atMostTwo: the processes
that have not caught up all sit on the same previous state, because a new edge
is only ever started from a quiescent cluster.  That is what
WaitForProcSignalBarrier() buys.


Assumptions
===========
These are the places where the model is weaker than reality, and where a bug
could therefore hide.

* One coordinator at a time.  Step.advanceShared requires the cluster to be
  quiescent, which encodes both the WaitForProcSignalBarrier() at the end of
  each SetDataChecksums*() call and the fact that only one process drives
  transitions: the launcher is a singleton on a primary
  (DataChecksumsState->launcher_running), and the startup process is the only
  replayer on a standby.

* Barriers are absorbed atomically.  The model has no notion of a backend
  observing shared memory part-way through a writer's update sequence.  This is
  justified by info_lck and by the fact that backends only ever read their own
  local copy.

* AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier() skips validation entirely when
  RecoveryInProgress().  The model always applies the table.  Standby safety
  therefore rests on the primary's edge sequence being one the table would have
  accepted, which is what coordinatorEdges_absorbable checks.

* WAL is not modelled.  A standby is abstracted as replaying the primary's
  sequence of edges, so xlog2_redo() and the XLOG_CHECKPOINT_REDO handling
  appear only as coordinator transitions.  In particular
  GetLastChecksumChangeRecPtr() and backup_checksums_verifiable() are out of
  scope: they are about whether a base backup taken across a transition can be
  verified, which is an LSN-ordering question, not a state-machine one.

* Liveness is covered only for the barrier handshake.  BarrierRetires says a
  pending barrier is always absorbed; nothing says the datachecksums worker
  makes progress, that a requested enable ever completes, or anything at all
  about the cost-delay throttling.  Making the first two checkable needs the
  launcher's goal -- the target state it was asked to reach -- to become part of
  the state, which is the obvious next extension of the TLA+ module.

* The TLA+ results are bounded.  They hold for the three backends and two pages
  in DataChecksums.cfg, not in general; that is what the Lean development is
  for.  Raising the bounds is cheap, and the state count stays in the
  thousands.

* Pages are abstracted to a single bit, "carries a valid checksum".  Torn pages,
  the pd_checksum field itself and the checksum algorithm are not modelled.


Building and checking
=====================
Neither model needs anything from the PostgreSQL build tree, and neither takes
more than a few seconds.

Lean.  The development is plain Lean 4 with no dependencies -- in particular it
does not use Mathlib -- so it builds offline:

    cd src/test/modules/test_checksums/spec
    lake build

lean-toolchain pins the exact compiler version, which is what the standard Lean
version manager, elan, reads:

    curl https://elan.lean-lang.org/elan-init.sh -sSf | sh

Alternatively, download the release named in lean-toolchain from
https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/releases and put its bin directory on PATH.

A successful "lake build" is the whole verification: there are no tests to run.
The development contains no "sorry" and no added axioms; the top-level results
depend only on propext, which can be confirmed with

    #print axioms Spec.reachable_readSafe

TLA+.  TLC needs only a JRE and tla2tools.jar, from
https://github.com/tlaplus/tlaplus/releases:

    cd src/test/modules/test_checksums/spec/tla
    TLC="java -cp /path/to/tla2tools.jar tlc2.TLC"

    $TLC DataChecksums
    $TLC -config Inductive.cfg DataChecksums

Both of those should report "Model checking completed. No error has been found."
The other two configurations are expected to fail, and their .cfg files describe
what the failure should look like:

    $TLC -config BrokenTable.cfg DataChecksums
    $TLC -config Livelock.cfg DataChecksums


Why two tools
=============
Lean delivers something no model checker can: the theorems hold for an arbitrary
number of backends and pages, not for three backends and two pages.
reachable_writeSafe is quantified over Backend and Page as opaque types, so
there is no state explosion to bound and no "we checked up to N" caveat.  Lean
also lets the finite parts of the argument -- the barrier table audits -- be
discharged by "decide", i.e. by exhaustive evaluation inside the proof, so both
styles of reasoning live in one artifact.  The negative results are a real
benefit of that mix: it is easy to state "adding this edge to the table breaks
safety" and have the compiler check it.

But this is a concurrency problem about interleavings and about a handshake
protocol, which is what TLA+ was designed for, and a maintainer is far more
likely to have seen TLA+ than Lean.  Concretely, TLC gives three things the Lean
development does not, and each is now a .cfg file in tla/:

* Liveness.  The livelock risk in AbsorbDataChecksumsBarrier() -- a rejected
  edge raises an error, procsignal retries, and WaitForProcSignalBarrier() never
  returns -- is a temporal property.  In Lean it can only be approached
  indirectly, by proving the coordinator never emits a rejected edge
  (coordinatorEdges_absorbable).  Livelock.cfg checks the real property under
  explicit fairness assumptions, and finding that the same defect also breaks
  safety, via InitLocalDataChecksumState(), is a result that fell straight out
  of writing it down that way.

* Counterexample traces.  When an invariant fails, TLC prints the interleaving
  that breaks it (BrokenTable.cfg).  A failing Lean proof prints an unsolved
  goal, which is much less useful for working out what the code actually does
  wrong.

* Cost of change.  Extending the model to cover, say, the LSN ordering behind
  backup_checksums_verifiable() means new invariant fields and new proof
  obligations in every case of Inv.step.  In TLA+ it means adding a variable and
  re-running TLC.

There is a fourth benefit that only appears once both exist: Inductive.cfg
model-checks the Lean invariant itself, so the two developments keep each other
honest.  If someone strengthens Inv in Lean to close a proof and the new
conjunct is not actually preserved by every transition, or if the Lean and TLA+
transition relations drift apart, that run fails.

Alloy would be a third option, well suited to the finite table audits, but
weaker than either for the temporal parts.

The division of labour to keep, then: Lean for the unbounded safety argument and
the barrier table audit -- the part most likely to catch a future edit to
checksum_barriers[] -- and TLA+ for liveness, for traces, and as a check on the
Lean invariant.


Files
=====
    Spec/State.lean      the four states and the reader API
    Spec/Barriers.lean   checksum_barriers, the coordinator edges, recovery
    Spec/Cluster.lean    the cluster and its transition relation
    Spec/Safety.lean     the table audits, the invariant, the safety theorems
    Spec/Trace.lean      a concrete execution, so the results are not vacuous

    tla/DataChecksums.tla  the same state machine, transcribed for TLC
    tla/DataChecksums.cfg  safety, the table audit, and liveness
    tla/Inductive.cfg      checks that Lean's Inv really is inductive
    tla/BrokenTable.cfg    counterexample: a shortcut edge in the table
    tla/Livelock.cfg       counterexample: a coordinator edge outside the table

